  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nSoutherly winds have been gusting up around 20-30 mph at times early \nthis morning behind the high surface pressure that moved out of SD \novernight. While these winds are expected to calm slightly during \nthe morning for most of the area, stronger gusts will stick around \nover the Prairie Coteau through the evening. This is because the \nsoutherly flow of the winds will cause downsloping winds to occur \nincreasing the wind speeds. There is a 40-60% chance for wind gusts \nto be at or slightly greater than 45 mph on the eastern edge of the \nPrairie Coteau, along the I-29 corridor, Saturday morning. These \ngusts will back off slightly during the afternoon and evening, \nthough they could still gust up to 40 mph at times.\n\nThe southerly winds will help to warm temperatures today and Sunday, \ncreating pleasant temperatures this weekend. Highs today are about 5-\n15 degrees warmer than normal over central SD and portions of \nnortheastern SD, getting up in the 60s to low 70s. Sunday will be \nabout 5-10 degrees warmer than normal over central and northeastern \nSD, with highs in the 60s. A cold front will be moving through \ncentral and northeastern SD and west central MN Sunday. While this \nwill not cause the high temperatures to cool too much on Sunday, it \nwill cause the lows on Sunday night and Monday morning to be in the \n30s over most of central and northeastern SD, except over the \nPrairie Coteau, which will be in the low 40s. These cooler \ntemperatures could cause some pockets of frost to form, especially \nover north central SD. Precipitation is not expected through the \nweekend and into the beginning of next week, as dry air has moved in \naloft over SD. This dry air will keep rain from forming as well as \nskies clear or mostly clear this weekend into the beginning of the \nwork week.",
